<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Île de la Cité</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Île de la Cité is an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the oldest core of the city. It is thought to have formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutetia">Lutetia</a>, the town of the Parisii, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it held the royal palace and the cathedral of Notre-Dame, begun in 1163. The Pillar of the Boatmen, a Gallo-Roman monument dedicated to Jupiter under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, was found beneath the cathedral in 1711.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite.jpg" alt="Île de la Cité" width="320" height="113" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DXR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C3%8Ele_de_la_Cit%C3%A9_shortly_before_sunrise,_West_View_140320_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
